(installed outdoors)\" for software version 5.0.1_ or higher\n  - \"B (installed in a building)\" for software version 4.3.1_ or lower\n  \n  This is a config-level variation (axis: software_version). Could emit config_level entry: attribute \"environmental_class\", axis \"software_version\", values: [{condition: \"5.0.1_ or higher\", value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"}, {condition: \"4.3.1_ or lower\", value: \"B (installed in a building)\"}].\n\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: varies by software version:\n  - -25 °C / +55 °C for software 5.0.1_ or higher\n  - -10 °C / +55 °C for software 4.3.1_ or lower\n  config_level: attribute \"ambient_temperature_range\", axis \"software_version\", values with value {min: -25, max: 55}, unit \"°C\", condition \"software_version >= 5.0.1_\", and {min: -10, max: 55}, condition \"software_version <= 4.3.1_\".\n\n  But the config_level values shape per rule 5: \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}\". So each value object: {condition, value: {min, max}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}.\n\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\". Value: string \"T50\"? Better: water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"} and maybe a separate: water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit \"°C\". The +0,1 °C → 0.1. I'll record both: water_temperature_class: \"T50\", water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C.\n\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa (16 bar). Value: 1.6, unit \"MPa\". Could add note about 16 bar. I'll add second characteristic? The primary: map_value_mpa: value 1.6 unit MPa. Maybe also \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\": 16 \"bar\". Hmm — 16 bar is parenthetical conversion. I'll include both: maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa, and maybe keep just one with value 1.6 unit MPa. To be safe include both as separate characteristics? The rules say split compound values. I'll include:\n  - maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"}\n  - maximum_admissible_pressure_bar equivalent: maybe skip. Actually I'll include it as \"maximum_admissible_pressure_equivalent\": 16 bar. Hmm, it's noise. I'll include just the MPa one. Actually parenthetical conversions are useful; but to keep clean, one entry with value 1.6 MPa. I'll include both to be thorough? Let me decide: keep maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa, and also record maximum_admissible_pressure with unit bar... can't duplicate attribute keys. I'll add \"maximum_admissible_pressure_alternate\" — no, too fabricated. Just MPa.\n\n- orientation: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\". Value string \"U0 and D0\", plus maybe a note. I'll record value \"U0 and D0\" and maybe a separate characteristic \"straight_length_requirement\": \"0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream\". I'll keep as one string value: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\". Hmm — structured better as: flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0\". And upstream/downstream straight lengths 0 DN. I'll do:\n  - flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0\"\n  - upstream_straight_length: 0 DN\n  - downstream_straight_length: 0 DN\n  That's reasonable.\n\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is designed to measure reverse flow\" → value: true or string. I'll record reverse_flow_supported: true, or value string. I'll do reverse_flow: {value: \"designed to measure reverse flow\"}. Better: \"reverse_flow_measurement\": {value: true}.\n\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp 63 (0,63 bar)\" → pressure_loss_class: \"Δp 63\" or value 63 with class. Record: pressure_loss_class: {value: \"Δp63\"} and pressure_loss: 0.63 bar. I'll do pressure_loss_class: \"Δp63\", pressure_loss_max: 0.63 bar.\n\n- power_supply: compound:\n  - \"3,6 Volts D-cell Lithium replaceable battery\" → battery voltage 3.6 V, type lithium D-cell replaceable\n  - Flexpower (software 5.0.1_ or higher):\n    - 3.6 V D-cell Lithium battery (non-replaceable)\n    - 9 ... 30 VDC\n    - 100...230 VAC / 50-60Hz (+/-2 %)\n  \n  Per rule 3, split:\n  - battery_voltage: 3.6 V\n  - battery_type: \"D-cell Lithium replaceable battery\"\n  - For Flexpower: dc_supply_voltage: {min: 9, max: 30} V\n  - ac_supply_voltage: {min: 100, max: 230} V\n  - power_supply_frequency: {min: 50, max: 60} Hz\n  - frequency tolerance ±2% — maybe skip or record tolerance.\n\n  I'll record:\n  - battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n  - battery_type: {value: \"D-cell Lithium\"}... plus replaceable vs non-replaceable depends on config (replaceable for battery-only; non-replaceable under Flexpower). Hmm, this is config dependent. Simplify:\n    - power_supply_dc_voltage: {min:9, max:30} V (condition Flexpower)\n    - power_supply_ac_voltage: {min:100, max:230} V\n    - power_supply_frequency: {min:50, max:60} Hz\n  I'll keep type_level entries simple.\n\n- field_current note: \"In case of software version 4.3.1_ or lower the Field Current can only be set to 16 mA. For software version 5.0.1_ or higher the different Field Currents can be selected and shall be set to 16 mA.\"\n  → field_current: 16 mA. config_level maybe. I'll record type_level: field_current: {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"mA\"}. Actually \"shall be set to 16 mA\" for 5.0.1+, and \"can only be set to 16 mA\" for ≤4.3.1 — effectively 16 mA in all cases. type_level: 16 mA.\n\n- Software versions and CRC checksums: This is a matrix table:\n  - versions 4.0.4_, 4.0.10_, 4.0.11_, 4.0.12_, 4.2.2_, 4.2.4_, 4.2.5_, 4.2.6_, 4.3.0_, 4.3.1_ → CRC: Not applicable\n  - 5.0.1_ → 4Cb5\n  - 5.0.2_ → 71d5\n  - 5.0.3_ → CFF7\n  - 5.0.5_ → dCAb\n  - 5.1.0_ → Ab62\n  \n  → matrix_tables entry: name \"Software versions and CRC checksums\", columns [\"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\"], rows per version.\n\n**Table 2: family of instruments** — the big model matrix.\n\nMeter sizes: DN25, DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600.\n\nEach size has multiple rows for accuracy class and flow rates. Let me parse carefully.\n\nThe table structure: Meter size | Accuracy class | Q1 | Q2 | Q3 | Q4 | Ratio Q3/Q1.\n\nDN25 (rowspan 2, class 2):\n- row 1: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.04, Q3=10, Q4=12.5, ratio 400\n- row 2: Q1=0.04, Q2=0.064, Q3=16, Q4=20, ratio 400\n\nThese two rows represent different Q3 values (10 m³/h and 16 m³/h) — different nominal flow variants within DN25. So each row is a distinct model variant: DN25 Q3=10 and DN25 Q3=16.\n\nDN40 (rowspan 2, class 2):\n- 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.25, 400\n- 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\n\nDN50 (rowspan 2, class 2):\n- 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\n- 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\n\nDN65 (rowspan 4):\n- class 2: 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\n- class 2: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n- class 1: 0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, 250\n\nDN80 (rowspan 4):\n- class 2: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n- class 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- class 1: 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160\n- class 1: 0.64, 1.024, 160, 200, 250\n\nDN100 (rowspan 4):\n- class 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- class 2: 0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400\n- class 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n- class 1: 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n\nDN125 (rowspan 4):\n- class 2: 0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400\n- class 2: 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\n- class 1: 1.5625, 2.5, 250, 312.5, 160\n- class 1: 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250\n\nDN150 (rowspan 4):\n- class 2: 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\n- class 2: 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\n- class 1: 2.5, 4, 400, 500, 160\n- class 1: 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\n\nDN200 (2 rows):\n- class 2: 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\n- class 1: 3.9375, 6.3, 630, 787.5, 160\n\nDN250 (2 rows):\n- class 2: 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400\n- class 1: 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\n\nDN300 (2 rows):\n- class 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n- class 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n\nDN350 (1 row): class \"1 or 2\": 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\nDN400 (1 row): class \"1 or 2\": 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160\nDN450 (1 row): class \"1 or 2\": 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160\nDN500 (1 row): class \"1 or 2\": 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\nDN600 (1 row): class \"1 or 2\": 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n\nTotal variants: 2+2+2+3+4+4+4+4+2+2+2+1+1+1+1+1 = let me count: DN25:2, DN40:2 (4), DN50:2 (6), DN65:3 (9), DN80:4 (13), DN100:4 (17), DN125:4 (21), DN150:4 (25), DN200:2 (27), DN250:2 (29), DN300:2 (31), DN350:1 (32), DN400:1 (33), DN450:1 (34), DN500:1 (35), DN600:1 (36). 36 model variants.\n\nModel IDs: DN25-Q3=10, DN25-Q3=16, etc. Better: use \"DN25/10\" or \"DN25_Q3=10m³/h\". I'll use model_id like \"DN25 Q3=10 m³/h\". Hmm, keep concise: \"DN25-Q3=10\". Each variant identified by size + Q3 (and class). Since for DN65, there are class 2 Q3=63, class 2 Q3=100, class 1 Q3=100 — the Q3=100 appears twice (class 2 and class 1). So model_id must include class too, or make class a model-level attribute. Since each variant is a combination of size, class, and flow rates, and class varies, I should encode model_id as size+Q3+class? Actually the two DN65 Q3=100 rows: class 2 with Q1=0.25 and class 1 with Q1=0.4. These are different variants (different Q1/Q2 for different class).\n\nApproach: model_id = \"DN65-Q3=100-class2\", etc. Or keep class as an attribute in model_level with values per model. Let me define model_id as e.g. \"DN25 Q3=10\" and include accuracy_class as an attribute in the model matrix. For duplicates (DN65 Q3=100 appears with class 2 and class 1; DN80 Q3=100 class2/class1; DN100 Q3=160 & 250 with classes; DN125 Q3=250/400; DN150 Q3=400/630), the size+Q3 combos collide across classes. E.g. DN80: class2 Q3=100 and class1 Q3=100 both exist. So model_id must include class: \"DN80-Q3=100-class2\" and \"DN80-Q3=100-class1\". But also DN80 has class1 Q3=160 with ratio 250 vs class2 Q3=160 ratio 400 — collide again. So include class in id for all: simpler to include class in every model_id.\n\nModel ID format: \"DN25-cls2-Q3=10\" or \"DN25 (class 2, Q3=10 m³/h)\". I'll use compact: \"DN25-class2-Q3=10\". For DN350-DN600 where class is \"1 or 2\": model_id \"D