e R137 shows both matrix_tables AND model_level.\n\nGiven the size, I'll do:\n- matrix_tables: Table 2 (full), Table 3, software CRC table.\n- model_level: q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio, accuracy_class, meter_size? meter_size is redundant with model_id but useful. I'll include: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio. That's 7 attributes × 36 values. Long but correct.\n- config_level for verification_scale_interval and indicating_range by meter_size group (since Table 3 groups sizes, and my models are size+flow combos, mapping to model would require repeating per flow variant; config-level by meter_size is the honest representation).\n\nNow let me build the model list with all data:\n\n1. DN25, class 2: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.04, Q3=10, Q4=12.5, ratio 400 → id \"DN25-class2-Q3=10\"\n2. DN25, class 2: Q1=0.04, Q2=0.064, Q3=16, Q4=20, ratio 400 → \"DN25-class2-Q3=16\"\n3. DN40, class 2: 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.25, 400 → \"DN40-class2-Q3=25\"\n4. DN40, class 2: 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400 → \"DN40-class2-Q3=40\"\n5. DN50, class 2: 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400 → \"DN50-class2-Q3=40\"\n6. DN50, class 2: 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400 → \"DN50-class2-Q3=63\"\n7. DN65, class 2: 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400 → \"DN65-class2-Q3=63\"\n8. DN65, class 2: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400 → \"DN65-class2-Q3=100\"\n9. DN65, class 1: 0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, 250 → \"DN65-class1-Q3=100\"\n10. DN80, class 2: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400 → \"DN80-class2-Q3=100\"\n11. DN80, class 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400 → \"DN80-class2-Q3=160\"\n12. DN80, class 1: 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160 → \"DN80-class1-Q3=100\"\n13. DN80, class 1: 0.64, 1.024, 160, 200, 250 → \"DN80-class1-Q3=160\"\n14. DN100, class 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400 → \"DN100-class2-Q3=160\"\n15. DN100, class 2: 0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400 → \"DN100-class2-Q3=250\"\n16. DN100, class 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160 → \"DN100-class1-Q3=160\"\n17. DN100, class 1: 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250 → \"DN100-class1-Q3=250\"\n18. DN125, class 2: 0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400 → \"DN125-class2-Q3=250\"\n19. DN125, class 2: 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400 → \"DN125-class2-Q3=400\"\n20. DN125, class 1: 1.5625, 2.5, 250, 312.5, 160 → \"DN125-class1-Q3=250\"\n21. DN125, class 1: 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250 → \"DN125-class1-Q3=400\"\n22. DN150, class 2: 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400 → \"DN150-class2-Q3=400\"\n23. DN150, class 2: 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400 → \"DN150-class2-Q3=630\"\n24. DN150, class 1: 2.5, 4, 400, 500, 160 → \"DN150-class1-Q3=400\"\n25. DN150, class 1: 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250 → \"DN150-class1-Q3=630\"\n26. DN200, class 2: 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400 → \"DN200-class2-Q3=630\"\n27. DN200, class 1: 3.9375, 6.3, 630, 787.5, 160 → \"DN200-class1-Q3=630\"\n28. DN250, class 2: 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400 → \"DN250-class2-Q3=1000\"\n29. DN250, class 1: 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160 → \"DN250-class1-Q3=1000\"\n30. DN300, class 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400 → \"DN300-class2-Q3=1600\"\n31. DN300, class 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160 → \"DN300-class1-Q3=1600\"\n32. DN350, class 1 or 2: 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160 → \"DN350-Q3=2500\"\n33. DN400, class 1 or 2: 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160 → \"DN400-Q3=4000\"\n34. DN450, class 1 or 2: 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160 → \"DN450-Q3=4000\"\n35. DN500, class 1 or 2: 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160 → \"DN500-Q3=6300\"\n36. DN600, class 1 or 2: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100 → \"DN600-Q3=6300\"\n\nModel IDs include class where relevant (all, for consistency). For DN350+ \"1 or 2\" → I'll write \"class1or2\". Let me use consistent format: \"DN25-c2-Q3=10\"? Better readable: \"DN25 (class 2, Q3=10)\". Hmm, commas inside JSON strings are fine. I'll use \"DN25-class2-Q3=10\".\n\nNow model_level arrays — these will be long. Each attribute with 36 values. Let me write them.\n\nActually, should I also include model-level \"label\"? Schema: models[]: model_id, label, attributes{}. Label could be \"DN25 electromagnetic water meter, class 2\". I can include labels briefly or skip. I'll include label as e.g. \"DN25, class 2, Q3=10 m³/h\". And maybe attributes with q-values too? That would duplicate model_level. The R137 example doesn't include attributes{} in models. I'll keep attributes empty/omitted and rely on model_level + matrix_tables. Or include attributes for direct per-model lookup — the schema says models[]: model_id, label, attributes{}. I'll add attributes with the key values (accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, verification_scale_interval_max, indicating_range_min). Hmm, that duplicates matrix_tables and model_level. To limit output size, I'll put the flow attributes in attributes{} per model AND keep model_level entries. Actually that's redundant but the schema seems to want model_level for varying attributes. Let me keep models[] lean (model_id, label) and put the matrix in model_level + matrix_tables, config_level for Table 3. That follows the R137 example pattern exactly.\n\nTable 2 matrix_table: name \"General characteristics of the family of instruments (Table 2)\", columns: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\"], column_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null], rows: 36 rows with model_id as key? Rows as objects per row. I'll include \"model_id\" too? The example rows use {\"size\": \"G2.5\", ...}. I'll use rows with meter_size etc. But since meter_size repeats, add \"model_id\" field for clarity. Description: \"Table 2 from certificate — flow characteristics per meter size and accuracy class\".\n\nTable 3 matrix_table: name \"General characteristics of the indicating device (Table 3)\", columns: [\"meter_sizes\", \"indicating_range_minimum_value\", \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\"], column_units: [null, \"m³\", \"m³\"], rows:\n- {\"meter_sizes\": \"DN25, DN40, DN50\", indicating_range_minimum_value: 99999999, verification_scale_interval_maximum: 0.0001}\n- {\"meter_sizes\": \"DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150\", ..., 0.001}\n- {\"meter_sizes\": \"DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450\", ..., 0.01}\n- {\"meter_sizes\": \"DN500, DN600\", ..., 0.1}\n\nSoftware table matrix_table: name \"Software identification\", columns [\"software_versions\", \"crc_checksum\"], rows:\n- {\"software_versions\": [\"4.0.4_\", \"4.0.10_\", \"4.0.11_\", \"4.0.12_\", \"4.2.2_\", \"4.2.4_\", \"4.2.5_\", \"4.2.6_\", \"4.3.0_\", \"4.3.1_\"], \"crc_checksum\": \"Not applicable\"}\n- {\"software_versions\": [\"5.0.1_\"], \"crc_checksum\": \"4Cb5\"}\n- {\"software_versions\": [\"5.0.2_\"], \"crc_checksum\": \"71d5\"}\n- {\"software_versions\": [\"5.0.3_\"], \"crc_checksum\": \"CFF7\"}\n- {\"software_versions\": [\"5.0.5_\"], \"crc_checksum\": \"dCAb\"}\n- {\"software_versions\": [\"5.1.0_\"], \"crc_checksum\": \"Ab62\"}\n\nOr one row per version: 10 rows + 6. I'll do one row per version for cleanliness. Actually the grouped row is faithful to the source. I'll do individual rows — easier for downstream. Hmm, \"rows[] (object per row)\". I'll do one row per software version: 16 rows.\n\nType-level characteristics summary:\n\n```json\n\"type_level\": {\n  \"measuring_principle\": {\"value\": \"Electromagnetic\"},\n  \"electromagnetic_environment\": {\"value\": \"E2\"},\n  \"water_temperature_class\": {\"value\": \"T50\"},\n  \"water_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n  \"maximum_admissible_pressure\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"MPa\"},\n  \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\": ... (skip)\n  \"orientation\": {\"value\": \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"},\n  \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\": {\"value\": \"U0 and D0\"},\n  \"straight_pipe_lengths\": {\"value\": \"0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream\"} — maybe as two: upstream_straight_length: 0 DN; downstream_straight_length: 0 DN.\n  \"reverse_flow\": {\"value\": true}? or string. I'll do \"reverse_flow_measurement\": {\"value\": true}.\n  \"pressure_loss_class\": {\"value\": \"Δp63\"},\n  \"pressure_loss_max\": {\"value\": 0.63, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n  \"battery_voltage\": {\"value\": 3.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n  \"battery_chemistry\": \"Lithium D-cell\",\n  \"field_current\": {\"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"mA\"},\n  \"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": \"1 and 2\"} — hmm. Maybe better as list? Schema: value must be number|{min,max}|string. \"1 and 2\" is fine. Or use \"value\": \"1, 2\". I'll use \"1 and 2\".\n}\n```\n\nWait — the pressure value \"1,6 MPa (16 bar)\": value 1.6 MPa. Also include equivalent in bar as separate characteristic \"maximum_admissible_pressure_equivalent\"? I'll skip to avoid clutter. Actually many extractors include it. The rule 3 shows separate characteristics for compound values. I'll include \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" 1.6 MPa only. Hmm, \"16 bar\" is genuinely useful. I'll add \"maximum_admissible_pressure_alt\" — no. Decision: include both as:\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_bar: 16 bar\nThe attribute names are distinct so no key collision. OK.\n\nFlow profile: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\":\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0\"\n- upstream_straight_length: {value: 0, unit_symbol: \"DN\"}\n- downstream_straight_length: {value: 0, unit_symbol: \"DN\"}\n\nConfig-level:\n1. environmental_class by software_version:\n```json\n{\"attribute\": \"environmental_class\", \"axis\": \"software_version\", \"values\": [\n  {\"condition\": \"5.0.1_ or higher\", \"value\": \"O (installed outdoors)\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"4.3.1_ or lower\", \"value\": \"B (installed in a building)\"}\n]}\n```\n2. ambient_temperature_range by software_version:\n```json\n{\"attribute\": \"ambient_temperature_range\", \"axis\": \"software_version\", \"values\": [\n  {\"condition\": \"5.0.1_ or higher\", \"value\": {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"4.3.1_ or lower\", \"value\": {\"min\": -10, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n]}\n```\n3. battery replaceability by power supply variant / software version:\n   - \"3,6 Volts D-cell Lithium replaceable battery\" (standard) or Flexpower with non-replaceable.\n```json\n{\"attribute\": \"battery_type\", \"axis\": \"power_supply\", \"values\": [\n  {\"condition\": \"battery-powered (standard)\", \"value\": \"3.6 V D-cell Lithium, replaceable\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"Flexpower, software 5.0.1_ or higher\", \"value\": \"3.6 V D-cell Lithium, non-replaceable\"}\n]}\n```\nHmm, keep it. Maybe fold into type_level power supply characteristics:\n- battery_voltage: 3.6 V\n- battery_type: \"D-cell Lithium\"\nAnd config for replaceability. I'll include the config entry.\n\n4. verification_scale_interval_maximum by meter_size group (from Table 3):\n```json\n{\"attribute\": \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\", \"axis\": \"meter_size\", \"values\": [\n  {\"condition\": \"DN25, DN40, DN50\", \"value\": 0.0001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150\", \"value\": 0.001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450\", \"value\": 0.01, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"DN500, DN600\", \"value\": 0.1, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"}\n]}\n```\n5. indicating_range_minimum_value by meter_size group — same value everywhere (99999999 m³), so type_level:\n   indicating_range_minimum_value: {value: 99999999, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}\n   Hmm — 99.999.999: is it 99999999 or 99.999999? Indicating range min value 99.999.999 m³ — a counter reading range. European thousands: 99,999,999. I'll use 99999999.\n\n6. field_current config? Both cases are 16 mA; software ≤4.3.1 only 16 possible; ≥5.0.1 selectable but shall be 16. So type_level: field_current: 16 mA is accurate. Add maybe a note? Skip.\n\n7. AC/DC power supply options config:\n```json\n{\"attribute\": \"external_power_supply\", \"axis\": \"power_supply_option\", \"values\": [\n  {\"condition\": \"DC supply\", \"value\": {\"min\": 9, \"max\": 30}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V DC\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"AC supply\", \"value\": {\"min\": 100, \"max\": 230}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V AC\"}\n]}\n```\nHmm — but rule 3 says split into separate characteristics. For type_level though these are Flexpower options (config). I'll put as separate type_level entries since both are listed options:\n- dc_power_supply_voltage: {min:9, max:30} V\n- ac_power_supply_voltage: {min:100, max:230} V\n- power_supply_frequency: {min:50, max:60} Hz\n- power supply options axis for battery vs Flexpower? Might overcomplicate. I'll do type_level with the values as listed options and note conditions in... can't note. Alternative: config_level with axis \"power_supply_option\":\n```json\n{\"attribute\": \"supply_voltage\", \"axis\": \"power_supply_option\", \"values\": [\n  {\"condition\": \"battery (internal)\", \"value\": 3.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"Flexpower DC\", \"value\": {\"min\": 9, \"max\": 30}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V DC\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"Flexpower AC\", \"value\": {\"min\": 100, \"max\": 230}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V AC\"}\n]}\n```\nThis is actually a nice representation. But rule 3's example shows type-level split for a single compound string. Here the power supply has alternatives (battery OR Flexpower variants). Config-level fits. But to follow the rule-3 pattern literally, I'd emit:\n- battery_voltage: 3.6 V\n- battery_type: \"D-cell Lithium\" (replaceable; non-replaceable under Flexpower)\n- dc_power_supply_voltage: {9–30} V\n- ac_power_supply_voltage: {100–230} V\n- power_supply_frequency: {50–60} Hz\n- power_supply_frequency_tolerance: ±2 %\n\nI'll go with the split type_level entries plus a config entry for battery replaceability. Simpler and matches rules.\n\nAlso \"Flexpower\" applies \"for software version 5.0.1_ or higher\" — could add footnote-like condition; I'll embed in battery_type config conditions.\n\nNow recommendation:\n- id: \"R49-1\" (canonical). edition: 2013. amendment: null. scheme: \"A\". accuracy_classes: [\"1\", \"2\"].\n\nCertificate:\n```json\n\"certificate\": {\n  \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"project_number\": \"2224507\",\n  \"page_total\": 5,\n  \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n  \"date_issued\": \"2021-01-26\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n}\n```\n\nissuing_authority:\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V.\",\n  \"address_lines\": [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"],\n  \"person_responsible\": \"M. Boudewijns\",\n  \"person_title\": null, (not given — omit)\n  \"phone\": \"+31 88 636 2332\",\n  \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n  \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n}\n```\n\napplicants: [{\"name\": \"KROHNE Altometer\", \"address_lines\": [\"Kerkeplaat 12\", \"3313 LC Dordrecht\", \"The Netherlands\"]}]\nmanufacturers: same.\n\ncertified_type:\n```json\n{\n  \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"WATERFLUX 3070\"],\n  \"module_designation\": null,\n  \"description\": \"An electromagnetic water meter for cold potable water and hot water\"\n}\n```\nHmm, description should be free-text from cert: \"An electromagnetic water meter\". I'll use that.\n\nmodel_family:\n```json\n{\n  \"family_name\": \"WATERFLUX 3070\",\n  \"models\": [ ... 36 entries ... ]\n}\n```\n\ntest_reports: 8 TERs + documentation folder:\n```json\n[\n  {\"id\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01\", \"date\": \"2009-09-16\", \"pages\": 41, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-10.01\", \"date\": \"2010-04-09\", \"pages\": 40, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-11.01\", \"date\": \"2011-05-02\", \"pages\": 40, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01\", \"date\": \"2012-03-28\", \"pages\": 40, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"NMi-13200194-01\", \"date\": \"2013-07-18\", \"pages\": 2, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"NMi-13200159-01\", \"date\": \"2015-03-18\", \"pages\": 6, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"NMi-15200645-01\", \"date\": \"2016-03-30\", \"pages\": 21, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"NMi-2224507-01\", \"date\": \"2020-12-30\", \"pages\": 27, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"T10201-9\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}\n]\n```\nHmm roles: the first four are likely Type Evaluation Reports; the NMi ones are additional test reports. It's ambiguous; the cert calls them \"associated report(s)\". I'll mark the first as type_evaluation_report and rest as test_report. Reasonable.\n\nShould pages include annexes? \"includes 41 pages and 14 annexes\". pages: 41. Fine.\n\nNow build model_level arrays. Attributes:\n- meter_size: string values (\"DN25\" etc.) — unit null.\n- accuracy_class: string \"2\"/\"1\"/\"1 or 2\". Could be numbers for numeric classes; \"1 or 2\" must be string. Keep all as strings for consistency? Mixed types ok? Values array items each have own value. I'll use numbers 1/2 where single, \"1 or 2\" string otherwise. Hmm, mixed types could be annoying but valid. I'll use strings \"2\", \"1\", \"1 or 2\" for consistency. Actually numbers are cleaner per rules (\"Numbers, not strings\"). But \"1 or 2\" is a string. Mixed within one attribute — acceptable. Let me use number when exact, string when ambiguous. Actually for consistency of downstream processing, I'll use numbers 1 and 2, and \"1 or 2\" for the ambiguous ones.\n\n- q1_minimum_flow: m³/h\n- q2_transitional_flow: m³/h\n- q3_permanent_flow: m³/h\n- q4_overload_flow: m³/h\n- q3_q1_ratio: dimensionless\n\n36 values each × 7 attributes = 252 value objects. Long but let's write them systematically.\n\nLet me tabulate (model_id | class | Q1 | Q2 | Q3 | Q4 | ratio):\n\n1. DN25-class2-Q3=10 | 2 | 0.025 | 0.04 | 10 | 12.5 | 400\n2. DN25-class2-Q3=16 | 2 | 0.04 | 0.064 | 16 | 20 | 400\n3. DN40-class2-Q3=25 | 2 | 0.0625 | 0.1 | 25 | 31.25 | 400\n4. DN40-class2-Q3=40 | 2 | 0.1 | 0.16 | 40 | 50 | 400\n5. DN50-class2-Q3=40 | 2 | 0.1 | 0.16 | 40 | 50 | 400\n6. DN50-class2-Q3=63 | 2 | 0.1575 | 0.252 | 63 | 7