ul) — hmm. The instruction \"rows[] (object per row)\". Either fine. I'll do per-version rows: 16 rows. Slightly long but precise. Actually, I'll keep the grouped row for v4 to reduce size: {\"software_versions\": \"4.0.4_, 4.0.10_, 4.0.11_, 4.0.12_, 4.2.2_, 4.2.4_, 4.2.5_, 4.2.6_, 4.3.0_, 4.3.1_\", \"crc_checksum\": \"Not applicable\"}. Then one row each for 5.x. That's 7 rows. Good compromise, faithful to source layout.\n\nTable 3 matrix rows as above.\n\nEverything assembled. Also add \"certificate.page_total\": 5.\n\nOne more check: \"certified_type.category\" — \"Water meter\". Rule says category examples include \"Measuring system\", etc. \"Water meter\" is right.\n\nmodule_designation: null. The certified type is a complete water meter, not a module. The cert doesn't mention modules. Set null (or omit). Schema shows field; I'll include null.\n\nDescription: \"An electromagnetic water meter\" — from \"Identification of the certified type\". Fine.\n\nType designations: [\"WATERFLUX 3070\"]. Any other type identifiers? No.\n\nOIML issuer id: \"NL1\" (from \"OIML Issuing Authority NL1\" and cert number).\n\nLet me also decide whether to include \"components\": no named sub-assemblies (sensor/indicating device implied but not typed) → omit.\n\nFootnotes: none → omit.\n\nRevision history: none → omit.\n\nFinal JSON structure:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {...},\n  \"issuing_authority\": {...},\n  \"applicants\": [...],\n  \"manufacturers\": [...],\n  \"certified_type\": {...},\n  \"model_family\": {...},\n  \"recommendation\": {...},\n  \"test_reports\": [...],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {...},\n    \"model_level\": [...],\n    \"config_level\": [...]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...]\n}\n\nOrder doesn't matter for parsing but I'll follow schema order roughly.\n\nWriting the model_level: each attribute object:\n{\"attribute\": \"meter_size\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [...36...]}\n{\"attribute\": \"accuracy_class\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \